#        <h2>The stages</h2>
#        <ul>
#          <li class="now"><span class="state">Now</span>
# -          <strong>Researching on-device sky segmentation, before anything is built.</strong>
# +          <strong>Two sky models tried on infrared; the choice between them is next.</strong>
#            The halo beside buildings and pylons under Aerochrome is the look's sky
# -          selection being wrong over whole regions: the sky inside a pylon's
# -          lattice left out, a pale building face taken in. Four refinements were
# -          tried and measured, including the one the published literature names,
# -          and none can fix wrong labels. Editors with clean sky edges start from a
# -          learned segmentation, so which models could run in the app on an iPad,
# -          offline, and whether they work on infrared, is being read now. Their
# -          maps come to you before anything is built.
# +          selection being wrong over whole regions, and no refinement fixes a wrong
# +          label. Two models that can be downloaded here and are MIT-licensed were
# +          run on six practice frames exported with the lens profile at 1, in the
# +          scratch lab only. Neither works on the frame as the app shows it:
# +          infrared makes the grass the brightest thing in the picture, and the
# +          models call it sky. Re-colouring the frame so foliage is green and sky is
# +          blue, from the frame's own colour axis, is what makes them work. The small
# +          model (u2netp) then finds the sky between the steel of a pylon and every
# +          wire, and calls a pale building face sky. The larger one (BiSeNetV2)
# +          knows the building and turns the pylon into a solid grey shape. The
# +          research, the numbers and the sources are in IR-SCIENCE 4b-x.
